{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nA rebar ticket carries no record of which tracker project it syncs into, nor which repos it belongs\nto. The current ticket state fields are seeded in `src/rebar/reducer/_state.py:29-81` and declared in\n`src/rebar/schemas/ticket_state.schema.json`; neither includes a project. Today the project is only\nimplicit — recoverable from a bound issue's key prefix in `bindings.json`, and absent entirely for a\nticket that has never been bound. Epic `0e68-41eb-5782-4336` needs it explicit, because a ticket that\nis deliberately not synced has no binding to infer from.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd two ticket state fields together, in one edit set: `bridge_project` (string) and `repos` (list of\nstrings). They are one story rather than two because a field is not usable without its write path,\nand splitting them would put two children on `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py`.\n\n`bridge_project` is **tri-state**, mirroring the established `file_impact_scope`\nundeclared/none/paths pattern (`docs/ticket-model.md:210-212`): absent means legacy, an explicit\nempty string means never sync, and a key means sync there. The three states are distinguished so a\npre-feature store is not read as \"every ticket is unsynced\". Resolution of the absent state is not\nimplemented here — it belongs to `resolve_project` in the mapping module.\n\nBoth fields are written into the CREATE event data by `create_core`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/composer.py:169-220`), projected by the reducer, and seeded in\n`make_initial_state`. Create-time arguments `--bridge-project` and `--repos` are added to all three\nwrite surfaces — CLI (`composer.create_cli`), library (`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:79-135`), and MCP\n(`src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:47-70`). Note the CLI flag loop rejects unknown options strictly\n(`composer.py:287-291`), so the new flags must be registered there or they are hard errors.\n\n`bridge_project` is **promote-only**, and — because a binding is written by the reconciler only\n*after* creation — this is an **edit-time** rule, not a create-time one: a genesis CREATE never sees a\npre-existing binding. Both fields are therefore also **editable post-create**, and the promote-only\nguard lives on the edit path, not the create path. Concretely: `bridge_project` and `repos` are added\nto the whole-field edit surface `edit_core` (`src/rebar/_commands/composer.py`, with matching\n`--bridge-project` / `--repos` edit flags in `edit_cli`), and projected on replay by the EDIT\nprocessor `process_edit` (`src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py`) — the same seam that already projects\ntitle/priority/description edits. Because the guard lives in the shared `edit_core`, all three\nsurfaces (CLI/library/MCP) inherit it, so no per-surface duplication is needed. The guard itself\nreads whether the ticket currently holds a binding via the single existing `bindings.json` reader in\n`src/rebar/_ids.py` (`_load_binding_reverse` / `binding_jira_key_map`) — a read-only dependency, not a\nmodified file: setting `bridge_project` on an **unbound** ticket is allowed, changing it on a ticket\nthat **already holds a binding** is refused with a non-zero exit and no state change. That keeps the\nchange free of any issue-move or unbind/rebind machinery. `repos` stays freely editable on the same\nedit path — it never reaches the tracker, so there is no binding to invalidate.\n\n**Out of scope here (named owner):** the parent's repos-field deliverable also includes *inbound\ndefaulting* — populating `repos` from the project mapping for a ticket arriving from a tracker. That\nis deliberately **deferred to the multi-project inbound-fetch child**\n`1734-7c6d-5a6a-48fd` (alias `algebraic-illstarred-mollusk`, \"Multi-project inbound fetch\"), because\nit depends on the mapping store the inbound fetch already consumes; this leaf owns only the field's\nschema/reducer projection and its create-time + edit write paths, not any tracker-driven defaulting.\n\nNo schema version bump: `docs/event-schema.md:124-150` states additive fields on an existing event\ntype do not require one. Types are generated from the schemas (`reducer/_state.py:11-13`), so the\ngenerator is re-run.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/schemas/ticket_state.schema.json`\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_state.py`\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py` — projects both fields from **both** the CREATE and the EDIT\n  events (`process_create` and `process_edit`)\n- `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py` — CREATE data + create-time args (`create_core`/`create_cli`)\n  **and** the edit surface carrying the promote-only guard (`edit_core`/`edit_cli`, `_EDIT_FIELDS`)\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py`\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py`\n- Read-only dependency (not modified): `src/rebar/_ids.py` `_load_binding_reverse` /\n  `binding_jira_key_map` — the single `bindings.json` reader the promote-only guard consults to\n  decide whether the ticket already holds a binding\n- tests under `tests/`\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard: a producer/consumer contract test for the event→state path (real\nCREATE event fed through the real reducer, no stubbing), plus a real CLI subprocess test for the\nargument parsing, since the strict unknown-option rejection is itself part of the mechanism.\n\n- Happy path: `create task \"t\" --bridge-project REB --repos rebar,api` produces a ticket whose state\n  shows `bridge_project == \"REB\"` and `repos == [\"rebar\", \"api\"]` after a full replay.\n- Three-state coverage: a ticket created without the flag replays with the field **absent**; one\n  created with an explicit empty value replays with `\"\"`. These must be distinguishable — asserting\n  the absent case is not merely falsy is the point of the test.\n- Cross-surface parity: the same ticket shape results from the CLI, the library, and MCP.\n- Failure path: an edit setting `bridge_project` on a ticket that already holds a binding is refused\n  with a non-zero exit and the stored value is unchanged; the same edit on an unbound ticket succeeds.\n- Collateral invariant: replaying a ticket created before this change yields identical state apart\n  from the two new keys.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `bridge_project` and `repos` are declared in the ticket state schema, seeded in\n      `make_initial_state`, and projected by the reducer; checked by a replay test asserting both on\n      a freshly created ticket.\n- [x] `--bridge-project` and `--repos` exist on the CLI, library and MCP create paths and produce\n      identical state; checked by a per-surface test comparing the resulting ticket state.\n- [x] The absent, explicit-empty, and set states are distinguishable after replay; checked by a\n      parameterized test asserting each of the three separately.\n- [x] Setting `bridge_project` via the edit surface (`edit --bridge-project`, promote-only guard in\n      `edit_core`) on a ticket that already holds a binding is refused with a non-zero exit and no\n      state change, while the same edit on an unbound ticket succeeds; checked by a paired test\n      covering both directions.\n- [x] A ticket created before this change replays to the same state apart from the new keys; checked\n      by a fixture-based regression test."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786675472929927001,"uuid":"c86df3b1-5acf-4457-ab78-e1f965a27fae"}